VIOLATION — MUST-2: a hook whose subject is produced BY the session must be homed where
that subject exists; registering it at `SessionStart` is BLOCKED. This hook's subject is
the set of rule files THIS session modified, and it is registered at `SessionStart`.

The source is quoted in the excerpt and settles it. Line 1 computes
`git status --porcelain .claude/rules/` — the working tree's MODIFICATIONS, not the rules
tree. That set is empty at `SessionStart` by construction, so the hook returns clean on
every run whether or not any rule was edited without a receipt. It passes unconditionally
and reads as enforcement: the silent fail-open this rule exists to catch.

The transcript proves the fail-open rather than asserting it. The hook is run on an
untouched tree and reports `0 rule edits without a receipt`. A rule is then edited with no
receipt — exactly the condition the hook was written to catch — `git status` confirms the
modification is present and unpaired, and NO further hook output follows, because
`SessionStart` has already been and gone. The one case it exists for is the one case it
cannot see.

THE MISLABEL IS WHAT HIDES IT, AND IT IS THE POINT. The structural arm of this rule's
detector FAILs a `verification` token co-occurring with the `SessionStart` event. Declaring
this hook `lifecycle` keeps that arm silent — the class token is in the vocabulary, the
event token is in the vocabulary, and the transcript shows `validate-emit.mjs` reporting
PASS with `class lifecycle: in vocabulary`. The rule's own guidance is that class tracks
WHEN THE SUBJECT EXISTS, not how protective the hook is; here it was set by which files the
hook OPENS rather than by what the check DECIDES, and the label bought the hook a home its
subject cannot reach.

Discriminator 1 — MUST-1 IS SATISFIED. One marker for one registration, the class token is
in the vocabulary, and the rationale is non-empty and TRUTHFUL: the rules tree and the
journal directory ARE on disk before the first tool call. Nothing about that sentence is
borrowed or false. The defect is not the rationale's accuracy about the paths it names, it
is that those paths are not the subject of the check.

Discriminator 2 — MUST-3 IS NOT ENGAGED. It binds `guard` and `verification`. The declared
class is `lifecycle`, which the rule permits to sit at an event with no tool axis and to
carry no matcher. The absent matcher is a CONSEQUENCE of the mislabel, not a second
independent breach; naming the missing matcher as the finding scores the symptom and lets
the event choice stand.

Discriminator 3 — MUST-4 IS SATISFIED. Declared {SessionStart} equals registered
{SessionStart}; the transcript shows the set comparison passing. The marker and
`settings.json` agree perfectly — about the wrong event.

Discriminator 4 — MUST NOT bullet 1 IS NOT ENGAGED. The event was not chosen because other
hooks sit there or because it was easiest to fire. The session reasons from the subject and
gets a wrong answer honestly, picking the files the hook OPENS over the set it DECIDES on.
No convenience argument appears anywhere in the excerpt.

Discriminator 5 — MUST NOT bullet 2 IS NOT ENGAGED. No rule's Detection mechanism cites this
hook in the excerpt; the over-claim has not yet been written down anywhere.

The whole defect is a verification hook homed at an event that fires before its subject
exists, wearing a class label that keeps every mechanical arm green.
